## One shared metadata and capacity owner

Use one authoritative canonical private localStorage journal value, not an independently updated index plus per-record values. It holds version, generation, records keyed by actual enrollment identity, identity/operation lookup metadata, and fixed capacity reservations. Capacity accounting adds reserved64KiB per slot plus measured canonical top-level/index metadata, rather than counting only each currently short payload. The lookup index is derived and validated from the records on every read; it never authorizes deletion, replacement, absence or submission. Scope of this journal is exactly browser Join custody. No generic outbox, claim journal or new storage backend is introduced.

All participants use mandatory Web Locks. Fixed order: acquire the enrollment lock first (normalized serving origin + full membership incarnation + keyID), then acquire the ONE origin-wide Join metadata/budget lock. Never acquire any enrollment lock while holding that metadata lock, and never acquire two enrollment locks at once. Same-identity lock may span native network/recovery; the global metadata lock covers only bounded read/validate/change/canonical write/readback and releases BEFORE every native/network await. A different identity can make progress while the first waits for a network answer.

Under the global lock, read the complete journal within its raw character and UTF-8 allowance BEFORE parsing; strict shape/signature/binding checks precede use. A new operation reserves one slot and its maximum record allowance before building/signing; the unsigned binding/reservation is kept first. Building then occurs under the enrollment lock and the completed exact envelope is written/read back under the global lock before dispatch. A crash after the unsigned reservation leaves a reserved-but-unprepared operation, not a permission to infer no earlier external history or recycle another record. Each slot reserves64KiB INCLUDING signed envelope, known-answer history, observations and selection metadata. The shared journal is at most64records and4MiB raw UTF-8 INCLUDING index/generation/reservation metadata. Capacity admission checks the canonical proposed whole journal's size and reserved-growth ledger;64times64KiB plus metadata cannot fit4MiB, so effective admission may be fewer than64. Do not call64 a guaranteed count. A per-record or journal overflow refuses before signing/POST; preserve every unresolved record and raw bytes. No evictions or implicit replacement.

Each record reserves growth before mutation: proposed64KiB splits into 32KiB original binding/envelope/credential-reference,16KiB native answer, 8KiB observation/selection and8KiB record/reservation metadata. Canonical UTF-8 serialization, including JSON escaping, must fit each allocation; a new-envelope bound failure sends nothing and retains its unsigned reservation until explicit never-dispatched release under this owner. The same64KiB reservation stays charged through acceptance and unknown outcomes; extra observations replace their latest observation only, never the original request or known native answer. Reject unsupported reply/metadata growth before dispatch when its declared maximum cannot fit. If an actual answer exceeds the reserved supported shape/size, preserve the original attempted request and in-memory known answer, report local retention unavailable, and do not admit another operation to consume its reservation. No truncation or dropped accepted fact earns a confirmed recovery claim.

Persist the whole operation prepared record/reservation in ONE setItem and verify readback while holding the metadata lock. Then persist attempted:true in another whole-journal write/readback immediately before POST. Readers treat either saved state conservatively; attempted may have been sent and must reconcile. Atomic replacement of one value avoids split record/index writes. Crash after preparation consumes a slot; crash after attempted consumes the full unknown reservation; crash after acceptance before answer write keeps attempted custody and settles the original request. On reload, recompute the ledger/index from validated retained records. A mismatch, corrupt/oversized journal, or partial legacy migration makes the journal unavailable; retain its original bytes and refuse new work. No reconstruction from missing fragments. If whole write succeeds but readback fails, local state is indeterminate and no POST begins; preserve the prior and candidate bytes in memory for explicit reconciliation. Cross-tab cooperating access is serialized; arbitrary same-origin scripts/storage tampering are outside this lock guarantee.

## Deliberate New and generation fencing

A person may explicitly choose NEW enrollment with an actual invitation and a newly generated/retained device key. This task plus the invitation's native authority is the legitimate start input; it does not assert first use or recover an earlier key/member. No POST/signing happens on startup or because a journal is absent. Preserve the current selection/key and every identifiable unknown record. A New action never replaces an unresolved same-identity operation; original-key settlement remains its only reconciliation route.

Before New, validate the actual serving origin, supported invitation pin/full refs and raw size without importing another key. Allocate a distinct operation ID and reserve one unsigned New slot under the shared metadata lock BEFORE key generation, using an operation ID until the key exists. Release that lock, generate/retain/verify a new uniquely named credential, then acquire its actual enrollment lock followed by the shared metadata lock to bind that same reserved slot to the resulting keyID and save the exact envelope. No path acquires an enrollment lock while holding metadata; the fixed order is unchanged. Local key creation performs no native/network work. Reuse neither a key from erased history nor a lost credential reference. If key storage is indeterminate, send nothing and retain the reserved New operation; it is not silently replaced. The native Join invitation judge remains authoritative for used/expired/revoked/wrong-target invitations; local validation cannot grant enrollment or infer the same member.

A validated existing journal retains its generation during deliberate New. If all journal history is absent, an explicit New creates a NEW random journal generation under the metadata lock and writes/readbacks it before reservation. Do not create/reset a generation during startup, an incoming answer or a corrupt-journal read. Corrupt retained history is preserved and blocks New until its owner resolves it; absence is disclosed as unknown prior history even after a distinct New is selected. A generation is local custody, not proof that earlier native operations did not occur.

Every continuation captures generation, actual identity, operation ID, exact reservation ID/allowance and original signed request digest/bytes. Immediately before POST and before saving any answer/settlement/observation/selection, the continuation that ALREADY holds its enrollment lock acquires ONLY the metadata lock and requires the CURRENT validated journal to match all those fields. It never recursively requests the enrollment Web Lock. After a close/reload or other new turn, acquire enrollment FIRST, then metadata; release metadata before network while retaining the one enrollment lock for that turn. Readback confirms the canonical update before continuing. An answer cannot recreate a missing journal/record/reservation, change generation, replenish capacity or overwrite another operation. If cleared/reinitialized/changed, retain the known native answer in memory only, report “Original answer received; local prior history/recovery unavailable” and preserve any current journal. Do not reinsert the old record or auto-select it. Unknown/accepted native effects still may exist outside lost local custody. The response guard is checked AFTER network completion; a pre-send guard alone cannot prevent late-answer resurrection after a clear/reset.

## Lost history is distinct from missing credential

A retained valid operation plus missing original key is identifiable: “Original enrollment retained; original key unavailable. No new enrollment was sent.” Fence that retained enrollment identity, preserve envelope/answer, and permit only native read paths the original custody still authorizes. Corrupt retained bytes mean “Local Join recovery record unavailable”; retain raw bytes and block new Join under the shared journal until repair. Do not pretend its unvalidated identity or acceptance is proven.

An absent/cleared journal AND absent index/key/history cannot identify an original operation or prove first use. Say “Prior enrollment history is unknown; local recovery is unavailable.” Do not claim one specific operation is retained/fenced, do not say no prior POST occurred, and do not generate a new key/signature or send Join merely because local storage is empty. A persisted first-use marker in the same storage is equally erasable and cannot solve this. Current first-use invitation UI must represent this missing history condition. It may offer the deliberate distinct New task adopted above; it must not label that task recovery or infer no previous enrollment.

External recovery requires independently retained original full binding, exact signed envelope or native fact/proof and ORIGINAL signing-key custody. Verify origin, directory/membership incarnation, actor/key, invitation and native pin before original-key settlement or identical resubmission. External proof of acceptance does not manufacture the lost private key or current access. No admin/operator key transfer, assumed same-member identity or fresh enrollment from an empty-store inference. The deliberate distinct New task does not satisfy the wider C4 same-member, second-device or lost-key recovery obligations; no new backend service or admin key transfer is adopted.

## Required two-identity, loss and generation witnesses

1. Two tabs with DIFFERENT membership/key identities race the final available slot/byte reservation. Both use their own enrollment lock, then the shared metadata lock. One atomic reservation wins; the loser reads the new ledger, refuses capacity before signing/POST, and leaves the winner's exact raw unknown record untouched. Repeat conceptually for answer growth while another identity reserves work: fixed maximum reservation prevents overcommit. This is one focused fixture with a deterministic lock gate, no sleeps or field sweep.

2. Crash gates before whole write, after prepared write, after attempted write and after native accepted reply show index/ledger recovery from the SAME canonical journal; no shared index lost update and no second native envelope. Global lock is released during network wait, verified by the second identity's bounded reservation/refusal completing while the first answer is held.

3. Distinguish valid record+missing key, corrupt record, and entirely absent journal/index/key. Only the first names a retained original enrollment; both latter cases cannot automatically POST and use different truthful recovery language. Entirely absent history permits only the explicit distinct New task with newly retained key/invitation; corruption still blocks New until resolved. Restore independently retained original envelope/key into the authorized recovery path, settle exact original acceptance, and never infer a new member.

4. Hold an original Join reply, clear its journal, explicitly create a different New generation, then release the old accepted/unknown/refused reply. It remains in memory with truthful local-history-unavailable status, cannot recreate or overwrite any current record/reservation/index and cannot select the old room. Also race a record-operation/reservation mismatch within one generation; no old answer is saved. Before-POST generation loss sends zero mutation.

5. Fresh/cleared browser startup sends nothing; deliberate New with actual invitation and verified new key creates one distinct exact request. Used, expired, revoked and wrong-target native invitations refuse through the native judge. Retained same-identity unknown requests stay fenced; no Lost history state is falsely presented as successful member recovery.

